\title{Set and bag expressions}

\p{Since types can be polymorphic in capabilities and entities, it's
not enough to just keep track of \em{e.g.} literal capability sets; we
need to keep track of variables that can stand for unknown
collections, with the ability to combine them with other collections.}

\transclude{00GY}

\p{
but quotiented by the assumption that #{\varnothing} is the identity for #{\cup}, and #{\cup} is associative, commutative and idempotent.  For example, #{(\varnothing \cup \delta) \cup (\{\sf{Lambda}\} \cup \delta) = \delta \cup \{\sf{Lambda}\}}.  We also write set literals like #{\{\Exc{\sf{if}}, \sf{Lambda}\}} as an abbreviation for #{\{\Exc{\sf{if}}\} \cup \{\sf{Lambda}\}}.
